WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.500
Keras.

2
00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:06.039
What took a hand-written loop in PyTorch becomes three declarative calls.

3
00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:07.200
Let's see the trade.

4
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:13.567
Keras is a high-level API that turns training into define, compile, and fit.

5
00:00:13.867 --> 00:00:19.200
Since Keras three, the same code can run on TensorFlow, JAX, or PyTorch underneath.

6
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.967
It's fast for standard architectures, and that's its whole appeal.

7
00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:25.467
Here are the three steps.

8
00:00:25.767 --> 00:00:28.367
Define the architecture as a stack of layers.

9
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:32.214
Compile attaches an optimizer, a loss, and metrics.

10
00:00:32.214 --> 00:00:33.884
No training happens yet.

11
00:00:33.884 --> 00:00:35.067
It's just wiring.

12
00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:40.033
Fit runs everything: batching, shuffling, validation, and progress bars.

13
00:00:41.233 --> 00:00:43.467
Here's a transit classifier in Keras.

14
00:00:43.767 --> 00:00:47.867
Three dense layers take flux features down to a single probability.

15
00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:51.567
Compile picks the Adam optimizer and a binary loss.

16
00:00:51.867 --> 00:00:55.567
And one fit call replaces the entire loop you wrote by hand.

17
00:00:56.767 --> 00:00:58.121
Keras isn't free, though.

18
00:00:58.121 --> 00:00:58.933
Let's weigh it.

19
00:00:59.233 --> 00:01:03.500
Keras wins on conventional problems where iteration speed matters.

20
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.933
Raw PyTorch wins when you need a custom loss, or control of the loop.

21
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:12.633
Knowing what fit hides is what separates an engineer from an API user.

22
00:01:13.833 --> 00:01:15.300
Here's the payoff of speed.

23
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:22.600
One afternoon fitting a dense network on two hundred flux features reaches eighty nine percent validation accuracy.

24
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.533
Why does this matter before building a bigger model?

25
00:01:26.833 --> 00:01:30.300
Before a week of custom CNN work, you fit this baseline.

26
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.000
It proves the signal is learnable, and gives you a number to beat.

27
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:38.033
Now the week of CNN work has a clear goal: six more points.

28
00:01:39.233 --> 00:01:40.119
Let's recap.

29
00:01:40.119 --> 00:01:42.333
Keras is define, compile, fit.

30
00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:44.600
Fit hides the loop you wrote by hand.

31
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:48.200
It's great for conventional problems and fast baselines.

32
00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:50.854
Drop to PyTorch for custom needs.

33
00:01:50.854 --> 00:01:52.567
Next up: the perceptron.
